-- Q19: PROC-19 stem truncated in DB (~500 chars)
UPDATE questions
SET question_text = $proc29$It has been decided that the project will be managed using waterfall. However, due to ambiguous requirements, the software will be developed in short iterations using Scrum. After acquiring a team from within the organization, the project manager realizes that the organization lacks technical expertise in several key areas and will require external resources from a vendor. The organization, however, wants to preserve its strategic discretion on the work performed. What is the best contracting technique for the project manager to maximize collaboration with the vendor?$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-19$proc29$;

-- ========== Explanation restores ==========

-- Q02: PROC-2 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$Agreements can be amended at any time before contract closure by mutual consent, in accordance with the change control terms of the agreement. The rights and obligations of both parties will be captured in the terms and conditions of the contract. The contract terms may or may not provide circumstances that provide relief from contractual obligations along with any associated penalties. Without knowing the terms of the agreement, of the choices provided, contract termination by the mutual consent is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: AEither party before the start of work Incorrect. Both parties are bound by the terms of the contract. Unless the contract specifically states that either party can terminate the contract before the start of work, the agreement is enforceable. Since the question does not provide any information on the contract terms, this is not the best answer choice. BThe buyer before the start of work Incorrect. The buyer may be able to terminate the contract before the start of work, but only if that right is provided in the agreement. The terms of the contract will also outline any penalties associated with early termination. The question does not provide any information on an early termination clause which makes another choice a better answer to the question asked. CEither party by mutual consent Correct. Agreements can be amended at any time before contract closure by mutual consent, in accordance with the change control terms of the agreement. If the early termination clause is not provided in the contract, this clause can be amended by mutual consent. DEarly termination is not an option Incorrect. Agreements can be amended or terminated by mutual agreement. The contract terms may also specify other circumstances in which the contract may be terminated before closure along with any penalties that may be associated with early termination.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-2$proc29$;

-- Q03: PROC-3 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$A bidder conference is a meeting between the buyer and prospective sellers before proposal submittal. They are used to ensure that all potential bidders have a clear and common understanding of the procurement and no bidders receive preferential treatment. Of the available choices, providing equal access to information best exemplifies the value of fairness. Details for Each Option: AResponsibility Incorrect. Responsibility is a duty to take ownership of the decisions one makes or fails to make, the actions one takes or fails to take, and the consequences that result. A bidder conference does not directly promote taking ownership of actions or decisions. BHonesty Incorrect. Honesty is a duty to understand the truth and act truthfully both in communications and in conduct. A bidder conference provides equal access to information but is not directly related to honest communications or conduct. CRespect Incorrect. Respect is a duty to show high regard for the entrusted resources. These resources may include people, money, reputation, the safety of others, and natural or environmental resources. Although holding a bidder conference does convey respect to the prospective bidders, the primary purpose of a bidder conference is to provide a clear and common understanding and ensure that no bidders receive preferential treatment. Therefore, of the available options, respect is not the best answer to the question asked. DFairness Correct. A bidder conference is used to ensure that all potential bidders have a clear and common understanding of the procurement and no bidders receive preferential treatment. Providing equal access to information is an example of the value of fairness.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-3$proc29$;

-- Q04: PROC-4 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$According to the scenario, the project manager has to begin the Close Project or Phase process. However, before doing this, contracts, if they exist on the project, should be closed as part of the Control Procurements process. Based on the scenario described, the project involves vendors. Therefore, when a project is terminated, contracts with the vendors should be closed. The procedures for formal procurement closure are defined in the contract and included in the procurement management plan. Closed procurements are an output of the Control Procurements process. Details for Each Option: AHave your attorney call the customer's attorney Incorrect. Without having more information, it is unnecessary to turn this situation into a legal issue by involving attorneys. BSchedule a meeting with the customer Incorrect. Meeting with the customer may be appropriate at some point, but the question asks for your first action. You have been formally informed by the sponsor that the customer has canceled the project. Therefore, meeting with the customer is not what you should do first. Making sure that the vendors do not spend their resources and do not bill the project that has been canceled is more important. CContinue doing project work until the customer notifies you directly Incorrect. The customer has already provided a formal letter and, therefore, is not necessarily obligated to notify the project manager specifically, so this would not be the best answer choice. DClose the contracts with the vendors Correct. If the project has been terminated, the project manager should first obtain financial, legal, and administrative closure. Closing contracts with vendors is part of this process.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-4$proc29$;

-- Q05: PROC-5 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$Sellers (whether vendors, suppliers, subcontractors, etc.) are considered outsiders to the company and therefore should be handled using the processes of the Project Procurement Management Knowledge Area. Knowing this distinction, the answer choices could be narrowed to the Conduct Procurements and Control Procurements processes. The Conduct Procurements process involves steps that end with awarding a contract. Monitoring that the work being performed meets contractual terms and issuing payment is part of the Control Procurements process. Details for Each Option: AControl Procurements Correct. The Control Procurements process has a financial management component that involves monitoring payments to the seller. This component ensures that payment terms defined in the contract are met, and that compensation is linked to the seller's progress as defined in the contract. BControl Resources Incorrect. The Control Resources process is concerned with ensuring that the physical resources assigned and allocated to the project are available as planned, as well as monitoring the planned versus actual utilization of resources and performing a corrective action as necessary. The Control Resources process does not handle procurement or payments. CControl Costs Incorrect. Since all project costs, including procurement costs, are monitored and controlled in the Control Costs process, this option could be a plausible answer choice. However, it is not the best option because the Control Procurements more accurately addresses the question asked. DConduct Procurements Incorrect. Conduct Procurements is defined as the process of obtaining seller responses, selecting a seller, and awarding a contract. It does not involve payments to sellers.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-5$proc29$;

-- Q06: PROC-6 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The scenario implies the project manager is carrying out the Plan Procurement Management process. Bid documents are among the outputs of this process. Bid documents are used to solicit proposals from the prospective sellers. An RFP is used when there is a problem in the project, and the solution is not easy to determine. This type of bid document is the most formal and has strict procurement rules for content, timeline, and seller responses. Since the project manager needs a highly detailed explanation of how the seller will perform the work, the most appropriate bid document to use in this situation is an RFP. Details for Each Option: ARequest for information (RFI) Incorrect. An RFI is used when more information on the goods and services to be acquired is needed from the sellers. While this document may partially address the needs of the project manager, there is another choice that provides a better answer. BRequest for advisement (RFA) Incorrect. 'Request for advisement (RFA)' is a fake type of bid document made up for this question. CRequest for proposal (RFP) Correct. An RFP is used when there is a problem in the project, and the solution is not easy to determine. This is the most formal of the bid document types and has strict procurement rules for content, timeline, and seller responses. DRequest for quotation (RFQ) Incorrect. An RFQ is commonly used when more information is needed on how vendors would satisfy the requirements and/or how much it will cost. Although some of the information the project manager is looking for can be included in the RFQ, another answer choice more accurately addresses the question asked.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-6$proc29$;

-- Q07: PROC-7 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$Contested charges where the buyer and seller cannot reach an agreement are called claims. The contract usually has terms to handle claims administration. If the parties cannot resolve the claim, it should be handled by alternative dispute resolution (ADR), typically following procedures established in the contract. There are two types of ADR, arbitration and mediation. Direct negotiation between the parties is the preferred method. Litigation in the courts is the last option that a project manager should choose. Based on the scenario described, the project manager has negotiated directly with the seller. However, all conversations have ended in an impasse. In this situation, the next step for the project manager to take is to identify some form of alternative dispute resolution. Details for Each Option: APay the vendor as some products were delivered Incorrect. If there are charges for which the buyer and seller cannot agree, they become claims. In the scenario described, you are disputing the cost for the final product they provided you, making it a claim. Typically, the vendor would be invoicing you regularly as products were produced. The vendor would have been compensated for the other products leaving only the payment of the final product unpaid. Since you are contesting the final product and previous invoices were most likely paid, this is not the best answer. BTake the vendor to court Incorrect. A claim is a contested charge on which the buyer and seller cannot agree. If the parties cannot resolve a claim themselves, the preferred method of resolution is through alternative dispute resolution. Using litigation and taking a vendor to court is the last choice for settling any claims. CDiscontinue further meetings with the vendor and hold back payment Incorrect. There is a contract in place between you and the vendor. Avoiding meetings or lack of contact is not going to end the contract. You need to try to resolve the area of disagreement, or claim, which cannot happen if you have no further contact with the vendor. DIdentify some form of alternative dispute resolution Correct. Contested charges on which a buyer and seller cannot agree are called claims. According to the scenario, you have tried to reach out to the vendor to no avail. When parties cannot reach an agreement on a resolution of a claim, it should be handled by alternative dispute resolutions.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-7$proc29$;

-- Q08: PROC-8 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The question implies that the Control Procurements process is being performed, and one of the organizational process assets associated with this process is prequalified seller lists. Prequalified seller lists contain potential sellers who are previously qualified (approved). These lists can be updated as an output of the Control Procurements process, and sellers can be disqualified and removed from the lists based on poor performance, such as the one described in the scenario. Therefore, of the choices provided, to ensure that this seller will not be considered again for future contracts, recommending the removal the seller from the prequalified sellers list is the best course of action for the project manager to take in this situation. Details for Each Option: ARecommend removing the seller from the prequalified sellers list Correct. Prequalified seller lists is an organizational process asset, which contains potential sellers who are previously qualified (approved). These lists can be updated according to the Control Procurements process, and sellers can be disqualified and removed from the lists based on their poor performance. BCapture the information in the issue log Incorrect. The scenario reads, "the project manager wants to ensure that this seller will not be considered again for future contracts," and the question asks, "What is the best course of action for the project manager to take in this situation?" Therefore, the question is ultimately asking about the best course of action to ensure that this seller will not be considered for future contracts. While the issue should be captured in the issue log, this action will not prevent the seller from bidding on future contracts. CUpdate the project management plan Incorrect. A project management plan does not contain information regarding specific sellers and would not prevent the seller from being selected for future projects. DConduct a procurement audit Incorrect. A procurement audit is a structured review of the procurement process. It does not contain information about seller eligibility for contracts.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-8$proc29$;

-- Q09: PROC-9 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The scenario describes a procurement process where the buyer is contracting a vendor and requesting them to deliver a product using agile best practices. Agile procurement is based on one of the Agile Manifesto values, "customer collaboration over contract negotiation". Unlike traditional projects, where the big emphasis is given on developing comprehensive contracts, agile projects value a collaborative approach to contracting. In this approach, both sides, the buyer and the seller, share risk and reward. One example is the incremental delivery with payment on incremental acceptance contract. This contract type stipulates that the vendor provides a working product increment that passed integration and testing on the vendor side, and is then tested by the buyer. Once the increment is tested and accepted by the buyer, the payment is issued. Details for Each Option: ANot-to-exceed with fixed-fee Incorrect. Not-to-exceed with fixed-fee contract type is typically used on projects with stable requirements. Based on the scenario described, the product is going to be delivered using agile methods. Agile best practices go hand in hand with changing requirements. Therefore, not-to-exceed with a fixed-fee contract would not be the best to be used in this situation. BFixed scope with incremental delivery and payment Incorrect. The second part of this option, "incremental delivery", makes this choice sound like a plausible answer. However, the scenario states that the vendor has been requested to deliver a product using agile best practices. This implies the buyer anticipates many changes throughout the project life cycle, making fixed scope type of contract unreasonable. CFixed price, fixed scope Incorrect. This answer choice can be eliminated because agile projects, like the one described in the scenario, are undertaken in the environment of changing requirements. Therefore the scope is unlikely to be fixed. DIncremental delivery with payment on incremental acceptance Correct. Incremental delivery with payment on incremental acceptance type of contract is used when a buyer chooses to pay the contracted company after each successful incremental acceptance.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-9$proc29$;

-- Q10: PROC-10 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The details of the services needed are not fully determined. Since little is known about how much time is needed for the consultant services, then a more logical choice of contract would be to use one that only pays for the time and work needed. Additionally, the materials and equipment needed are less known in this situation. Defining a contract that pays a fixed fee for open-ended services is too risky. Similarly, a cost reimbursable for a service with unknown boundaries could present its own risks – depending on how the contract was written and its terms and conditions. Ergo, in this situation, the best contract type would be the time and material contract. Details for Each Option: ACost plus fixed fee Incorrect. At this time, not enough is known to determine the costs and fees of the services requested properly. Additionally, the project needs procured services in the near term, so the project team may not have the time and resources available to define these materials and associated costs fully. BTime and material Correct. Since little is known on how much time and material is needed for the consultant services the time and material contract should be used. This contract type provides the most flexibility for each party to acquire the resources and services needed without pre-determining too many of the costs and other fees. CCost plus award fee Incorrect. Paying for the procured services via a cost-reimbursable type of contract may be warranted in some situations. Although in this situation, there are too many unknown costs and length of services required. Paying for the costs incurred would work if more were known about the expected costs. The inclusion of an award fee would not be useful in this situation unless more were known of the expected outcomes and satisfaction criteria. DFixed fee with economic price adjustments Incorrect. Paying a set cost for open-ended services is not a logical choice. That places too much risk on both parties – seller and buyer. The inclusion of economic price adjustments is more suitable for a long-term contract where fluctuations in costs, resources, commodities, market value, currency rates, for example, are likely. There is no indication in this scenario that a long-term agreement is needed.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-10$proc29$;

-- Q11: PROC-11 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The question suggests that the project team is undertaking the Plan Procurement Management process. One of the techniques used in this process is data gathering, such as market research. Market research includes the examination of industry and specific seller capabilities. The project team may leverage information gained at conferences, online reviews, and a variety of sources to identify market capabilities. The project team may also refine specific procurement objectives to leverage maturing technologies while balancing risks associated with the breadth of sellers who can provide the desired materials or services. Details for Each Option: ARequirements documentation Incorrect. Requirements documentation is a description of how individual requirements meet the business need for the project. Requirements documentation is an input to the Plan Procurement Management process rather than a tool or technique. BData gathering Correct. Market research is an example of a data-gathering technique. Market research includes the examination of industry and specific seller capabilities. The project team may leverage information gained at conferences, online reviews, and a variety of sources to identify market capabilities. CVendor differential analysis Incorrect. 'Vendor differential analysis' is a fake term made up for this question. DMake-or-buy analysis Incorrect. Make-or-buy analysis is used to determine whether the project deliverables should be produced internally or procured from outside the organization. The question suggests that the project team will be selecting a vendor to provide the deliverables. Therefore, the make-or-buy decision has already been made in favor of procurement.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-11$proc29$;

-- Q12: PROC-12 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$Source selection criteria are a set of measurable attributes desired by the buyer which a seller is required to meet or exceed to be selected for a contract. By focusing on source selection criteria, a proposal can be evaluated quantitatively, thereby removing any personal biases. Details for Each Option: AProcurement statement of work (SOW) Incorrect. The procurement SOW provides sellers with a clearly stated set of goals, requirements, and outcomes from which they can produce a proposal for the work they will provide. In this scenario, the sellers' proposals have already been received. Therefore, this is not a correct answer. BBid documents Incorrect. Bid documents are used to solicit proposals from prospective sellers. In this scenario, the vendors' proposals have already been received. Therefore, this is not the correct answer. CSource selection criteria Correct. Source selection criteria are measurable and provide a quantitative basis for evaluation of proposals thereby minimizing personal biases. The source selection criteria are the best thing for your project team to focus on when choosing the vendor. DProcurement management plan Incorrect. The procurement management plan details the activities to be undertaken during the procurement process. Source selection criteria are part of the procurement documents, not part of the procurement management plan. Therefore, this is not a correct answer.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-12$proc29$;

-- Q13: PROC-13 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$It can be inferred from the question that the Control Procurements process had been performed and procurements closed out as part of the process. One of the outputs of the Control Procurements process is procurement documentation updates. Procurement documentation includes all documents used in signing, executing, and closing an agreement. Included among these documents will be the invoices and payment records for the project. Based on the scenario described by the question, the next action for the project manager should be to check the procurement documentation for invoices and payment records. Details for Each Option: ATell the vendor that all invoices have been paid since the procurements have been closed Incorrect. While closed procurements imply that the corresponding invoices have likely been paid, the project manager should research the matter further to be certain of the invoices' status. This research will guide the project manager with the next step: either pay the invoice or provide the vendor with the proof of payment. BCheck the records in the procurement documentation Correct. Updates to procurement documentation are among the outputs of the Control Procurements process. This documentation includes all documents used in signing, executing, and closing an agreement. Included among these documents will be the invoices and payment records for the project. CReach out to the accounts payable department to see if anyone remembers paying the invoice Incorrect. While it is possible that someone in accounts payable would remember paying a particular invoice, this approach relies on someone's memory (assuming the relevant person even still works in the company). Finding proof of payment to settle the dispute with the vendor is a better course of action. DProceed with claims administration to resolve the dispute Incorrect. Proceeding with claims administration is a premature action. Reviewing the invoices and payment records in the procurement documentation should be done prior to taking actions related to claims administration.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-13$proc29$;

-- Q14: PROC-14 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$Force majeure is a common clause in contracts, which essentially frees both parties from liability or obligation when an extraordinary event or circumstance beyond the control of the parties, such as a war, strike, riot, crime, or an event described by the legal term 'act of God' (e.g., flooding, earthquake, volcanic eruption) prevents one or both parties from fulfilling their obligations under the contract. The topic of this question is not covered in the PMBOK® Guide. However, the Project Management Professional (PMP)® Examination Content Outline (ECO) indicates that while there are some commonalities between the PMBOK® Guide and the ECO, the exam is not bound by the PMBOK® Guide. The list of enablers specified in the tasks of the ECO domains is not exhaustive either. The ECO assumes that prospective PMP aspirants are familiar with other sources of information/preparation, including but not limited to the PMI Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct, PMI's Practice Standards (e.g., Scheduling, Earned Value Management, etc.), commonly frowned upon project management practices, such as gold plating, and others. We intentionally have these questions in our simulator so that you would be better prepared for the real exam. PMP aspirants are encouraged to explore additional sources of information and/or to be familiar with them from their project management experience. Details for Each Option: AForce majeure clause Correct. 'Force majeure' is a common contractual clause that frees parties, bound by a contract, from liability or obligation when certain circumstances beyond their control arise making performance impossible. Examples of force majeure include war, riot, strikes and 'acts of God' such as flooding, tornado, hurricanes, etc. BEscape clause Incorrect. An escape clause in a contract allows for an exit route from the contract without being liable for breach of contract. 'Subject to inspection' is an example of an escape clause in real estate purchase contracts. This clause allows the buyer to 'escape' from the contract with the seller (a vendor) if an inspection reveals any irregularities or defects. In this scenario, however, the tornado, or a force majeure, was the cause of the vendor to be unable to follow through on their contractual obligations. While an escape clause could also be used in this scenario, a force majeure clause more accurately addresses the situation described in the question and therefore, is a better answer choice. Whenever two answer choices seem to be correct, the option that most accurately addresses the question asked is typically the right answer. CArbitration clause Incorrect. An arbitration clause in a contract requires the parties to resolve their conflicts by arbitration making this answer incorrect. DGold clause Incorrect. A gold clause in a contract allows the seller to have the option to be paid in gold or gold equivalent which clearly is not what described in this scenario.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-14$proc29$;

-- Q15: PROC-15 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The party that awards the contract is responsible for declaring its closure. The buyer, usually through its authorized administrator, provides the seller with the formal written notice that the contract has been completed. Procurement closure only occurs after the deliverables have been accepted and transferred, all claims are settled, and final payments have been made. Details for Each Option: AThe buyer is responsible for closing out the procurement contract. Correct. The buyer provides the seller with the formal written notice that the contract has been completed. BThe seller is responsible for closing out the procurement contract. Incorrect. The seller does not close out the contract. The buyer closes out the contract and sends formal written notice to the seller that the contract has been completed. CBoth buyer and seller are mutually responsible for closing out the contract. Incorrect. Both the buyer and seller are mutually bound by the terms of the contract, but it is the buyer's responsibility to provide formal written notice of contract completion. DA procurement contract is considered closed when the deliverables are accepted. Incorrect. The contract is not closed out when the deliverables are accepted. Any outstanding claims should be settled, and final payment should be made before formal procurement closure.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-15$proc29$;

-- Q16: PROC-16 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$Companies that use agile best practices for their projects to deliver their products or services to other companies who act as buyers, i.e. customers, have to make sure that the buyers are familiar with agile delivery methods. One of the key elements on agile projects is continuous customer involvement and feedback. If customer involvement is a key, then the contract should include terms such as what input is required from the customer, how frequently the input is needed, what meetings the customer should be participating in, and so on. Details for Each Option: AAdding a clause allowing the buyer to cancel the contract if the vendor fails to deliver as agreed Incorrect. A clause that allows the buyer to cancel the contract if the vendor fails to deliver as agreed is not unique to agile projects. Also, such clause would benefit the buyer rather than the seller. BUsing standard form contracts recommended by the project management office Incorrect. There is no information provided to imply that standard forms would make the most sense in this situation. CClearly stating the need for customer involvement Correct. The requirements for customer involvement should be clearly stated in the contract because Agile is predicated on continuous feedback and involvement of the customer. DInsisting on legal involvement Incorrect. Legal involvement may not always be necessary.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-16$proc29$;

-- Q17: PROC-17 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$When a seller has provided the product or service defined by the procurement contract, the procurement is closed. Requirements for formal procurement closure are usually specified in the terms and conditions of the agreement and are included in the procurement management plan. Typically, to close a procurement, all deliverables should have been provided and inspected to ensure they meet technical and quality requirements, there should be no outstanding claims or invoices, and all final payments should have been made. In the final step of closing a procurement contract, the buyer provides the seller with formal written notice that the contract has been completed. Details for Each Option: APerform a procurement audit Incorrect. Procurement audits are performed during the life of the procurement contract to ensure the procurement is complying with organizational policies, procedures, and processes. In this scenario, the contract is in the process of being finalized so an audit would typically have been done earlier. Therefore, this is an incorrect answer. BClose the project Incorrect. The contract for the point of sale systems is being finalized. There is no indication that the entire project is complete. Therefore, closing the project is not the best answer. CProvide the seller with formal written notice that the contract has been completed Correct. In this scenario, you have inspected the completed installation of the point of sale systems, indicating the procurement is being formally closed. To complete procurement closure, the buyer provides the seller with formal written notice that the contract has been completed. DIssue a work performance report Incorrect. Work performance reports are the organized physical representation of work performance information. Work performance reports are distributed, as an output from Monitor and Control Project Work, to stakeholders who need to receive the information. In this scenario, you are in the process of completing a procurement. Therefore, issuing a work performance report is not your next step.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-17$proc29$;

-- Q18: PROC-18 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$It can be inferred from the scenario, that the project is in the Control Procurements process. Change requests are an input to this process and can include modifications to the terms and conditions of the contract, including the procurement statement of work, pricing, and descriptions of the products, service, or results to be produced. The payment schedule is typically specified in the terms and conditions of the contract. Based on the scenario, it is reasonable to assume that the contract specifies that the payments are to be released at the end of each month. Now, as the procurement procedures in the buyer's organizations have changed, the contract should be updated with the new payment schedule. The change should be agreed upon by both parties. According to the scenario, the seller welcomes the change, implying the seller agrees. Therefore, the next logical step for the seller is to ask the buyer to issue a change request to modify the contract. Details for Each Option: ADo nothing as the change is beneficial Incorrect. The payment schedule is typically specified in the terms and conditions of the contract. Regardless of whether the change is beneficial or not, it should be documented as a formal change request and, if approved, implemented in the contract by either amending it or modifying its terms and conditions. Doing nothing may harm both parties if contested changes around payments arise. BProvide the deliverables at the beginning of each month Incorrect. There is not enough information in the scenario to determine what is the current timetable of the deliverables and whether or not the change to the payment schedule will also require the seller to change the timing of their deliverables. CUpdate the procurement management plan Incorrect. On projects involving procurements, the buyer develops the procurement management plan. The question is asking what the seller should do. The seller is not supposed to update the procurement management plan of the buyer. The seller may have their own procurement management plan to handle the procurements with their third parties. However, this information is not provided, and the way the question is worded implies this answer choice refers to the buyer's plan. DAsk the buyer to issue a change request to the contract Correct. Approved change requests are inputs to the Control Procurements process described in the scenario. A change to the terms and conditions of the contract, such as the modification of the payment schedule, should be documented as a formal change request. If the request is approved, the contract will be updated.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-18$proc29$;

-- Q19: PROC-19 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The scenario implies the project, as a whole, is managed in a hybrid project management approach: waterfall and Scrum. Scrum is one of the agile methods. Agile methods place a great emphasis on collaboration, a sentiment reflected in the Agile Manifesto that values customer collaboration over contract negotiation. Agile methods are typically selected for projects where the project scope cannot be well-defined at the start of the project and will be progressively elaborated as the project progresses. As a result, collaboration becomes a critical factor in the project's success. In this scenario, the question is asking for the contracting technique that will maximize collaboration with the vendor. With a team augmentation approach, the supplier's services are embedded directly into the customer's organization. Having the development team comprised of internal and external resources, working as a cohesive unit, best supports the collaborative efforts. Details for Each Option: ATeam augmentation Correct. Team augmentation is an approach where the supplier's services are embedded directly into the customer's organization. Colocating the supplier's services with the customer will enhance collaboration. BEarly cancellation option Incorrect. Including an early cancellation option in the contract could be a good idea for a project where the software is developed in short iterations, as in the scenario described. However, the question is specifically asking about how to maximize collaboration with the vendor. An early cancellation option has nothing to do with enhancing collaboration. CFixed-price increments Incorrect. A fixed-price increments contracting technique allows the buyer to decompose the project scope into fixed-price micro-deliverables. This technique provides benefits to both the buyer and the seller/vendor, such as control over how the money is spent (for the buyer) and limiting the financial risk (for the seller). Maximizing collaboration with the seller/vendor, however, is not the main goal of this technique. DNot-to-exceed time and materials Incorrect. The main advantage of the not-to-exceed time and materials contracting technique is to provide the buyer with room for new ideas and innovations that were not planned initially rather than increase collaboration with the seller/vendor.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-19$proc29$;

-- Q20: PROC-20 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The lessons learned register is a project document used to record knowledge gained during a project so that it can be used to improve the performance of the current project. At the end of the project, the information contained in the lessons learned register is then transferred to an organizational process asset called the lessons learned repository for use in future projects. Any information regarding claims and how they were resolved should be documented in the lessons learned register to avoid recurrences in the future. Note, the question does not explicitly mention future projects. It reads, "What is the best way to prevent this issue from recurring?" Even though future projects are not mentioned, it can be implied. Additionally, the question suggests the current project is over. Therefore, preventing this issue from recurring infers recurring in future projects. Therefore, of the available choices, capturing the knowledge gained in the lessons learned register is the best way to prevent the problem from recurring. Details for Each Option: ARecord the final resolution of the problem in the issue log Incorrect. It is not known from the question whether the problem was captured in the issue log. Assuming it was, then the final resolution should be recorded as a project document update. However, the issue log will not make the information available for use in future projects, so this action is not the best way to prevent a recurrence of the issue. BCapture the situation and its resolution in the lessons learned register Correct. Information regarding any claims and how they were resolved should be documented in the lessons learned register to avoid recurrences in the future. CUpdate the risk register with the implementation of the risk response Incorrect. The question does not specify if the risk of a computer virus was identified earlier in the project. If this risk was captured in the risk register, then it may be updated with status changes and finally closed out. However, the risk register from the current project does not serve as an input for future projects, and the knowledge gained would be lost. DConduct future software development projects with only internal resources Incorrect. Using only internal resources would allow more control over the project. However, the performing organization may not have internal resources with the necessary skillset. Additionally, a computer virus could still be an issue even with using internal resources. Therefore, just using in-house developers is not likely to prevent the problem from occurring in the future.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-20$proc29$;

-- Q21: PROC-21 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$In the scenario described you are in the Conduct Procurements process having completed the vendor selection process and awarded a contract. The next process in the Project Procurement Management Knowledge Area is the Control Procurements process where the contract performance is monitored and controlled. All the answer choices, except performance reviews, describe tools used during the Plan Procurement Management process which precedes the Conduct Procurement process. Performance reviews are used to measure, compare, and analyze characteristics of the procured item and compare it against the vendor agreement. It is the only tool listed from the Control Procurements process making it the correct answer choice. Details for Each Option: AMake-or-buy analysis Incorrect. A make-or-buy analysis is used during the Plan Procurement Management process to determine whether work or deliverables can best be accomplished by the project team or should be purchased from outside sources. In this scenario, you have obtained vendor responses, selected a vendor, and awarded a contract which are all activities performed in the Conduct Procurements process. Since the Plan Procurement Management process precedes the Conduct Procurements process, a make-or-buy analysis would have already been completed at this point in the project life cycle. BPerformance reviews Correct. According to the scenario, you have obtained vendor responses, selected a vendor, and awarded a contract, all of which are activities that are performed as part of the Conduct Procurements process. The next step in the Project Procurement Management Knowledge Area would be the Control Procurement process where contract progress is monitored using performance reviews. CProcurement statement of work (SOW) Incorrect. The SOW describes the item being procured and is shared with prospective vendors so that the vendors can determine if they are capable of producing the deliverable. The procurement SOW is developed during the Plan Procurement Management process which precedes the activities of obtaining vendor responses, selecting a vendor and awarding a contract, described in the scenario. Therefore, this is not the correct answer. DDevelop source selection criteria Incorrect. Source selection criteria are developed during the Plan Procurement Management process to be used as evaluation criteria during vendor selection. In the scenario described, you are in the Conduct Procurements process and have already selected a vendor. Therefore, the source selection criteria would have already been developed, making this an incorrect answer.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-21$proc29$;

-- Q22: PROC-22 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The question states that you are considering a vendor to deliver some of the work packages for your project, implying you are in the Conduct Procurements process. Agreements (or procurement agreements) are an output from this process. Based on the scenario described, so far only informal discussions between your organization and one of the prospective sellers, Unique Foods, took place. Without any formalized procurement agreement between your project and the seller, there is no requirement for supplying funds or resources to the potential seller. Project managers are responsible for managing the costs of the project. Handing over funds to an outside party based on the prospect of procuring materials or resources from them is the unwise management of the project funds. Details for Each Option: ARequest Unique Foods to wait until procurement documents are prepared Correct. Verbal notification or acknowledgment without proper procurement documentation is not sufficient for a potential seller to proceed or produce materials. BSchedule a meeting and go over procurement deliverables and timing Incorrect. Until there are formal agreements in place, it may be too premature to meet for scheduling and logistics. CAssess the Unique Food Inc. requirements and develop a project scope statement Incorrect. The project scope statement is the description of the project scope, major deliverables, and exclusions. This document is developed by the performing organization to satisfy the requirements of the customer. However, in the scenario described, Unique Foods is your seller, not your customer. Therefore, if they are selected, your organization has to provide them with the procurement documents and a procurement statement of work, not with the project scope statement. DStart payments so resources can be procured Incorrect. There are no procurement agreements between you and the seller. There are currently discussions to come to an agreement, but nothing is established at this point.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-22$proc29$;

-- Q23: PROC-23 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The scenario suggests the project has been closed. In other words, the Close Project or Phase process is now complete. The final report is one of the outputs of this process. The final report includes information such as a summary level description of the project, a summary of any risks and issues encountered and how they were addressed, a summary of how the final product, service or result was achieved, and more. Since the vice president wants a summary overview of project results, providing the vice president with the final report will likely meet their needs and is, therefore, of the choices provided, the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: ALessons learned repository Incorrect. The lessons learned repository is a store of historical information about lessons learned from projects. Even though the lessons learned repository includes the final lessons learned and knowledge gained throughout the project, the vice president wants a summary of project performance, not lessons learned. The summary of project performance is typically found in the final report and not the lessons learned repository. Therefore, this choice is not the best answer to the question asked. BProject closure documents Incorrect. The project closure documents consist of formal documentation on completion of the project and the information on project deliverables transferred to the ongoing organization. The project closure documents do not contain a summary of project performance, making this choice an incorrect answer. CFinal lessons learned register Incorrect. The final lessons learned register is finalized during project closure, before being entered in the lesson learned repository. Even though the final lessons learned register includes the final lessons learned and knowledge gained throughout the project, the vice president wants a summary of project performance which is found in the final report and not the final lessons learned register. DFinal report Correct. The final report provides a summary of the project performance. The report includes information such as a summary level description of the project, a summary of any risks and issues encountered and how they were addressed, a summary of how the final product, service or result was achieved, etc. Therefore, providing the final report to the vice president would address her needs, making this choice the best answer to the question asked.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-23$proc29$;

-- Q24: PROC-24 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$Control Procurements is the process of managing procurement relationships, monitoring contract performance, and closing out contracts. The scenario is vague as to whether you are ready to close contracts; it only asks which activities are performed in the Control Procurements process. It's important to recognize that in the case of procurement management, closing contracts is part of the processes performed in the Monitoring and Controlling Process Group and not part of the Close Project or Phase process in the Project Integration Management Knowledge Area. Therefore, closing contracts with sellers is part of Control Procurements and, hence is the correct answer. Details for Each Option: AClose contracts with sellers Correct. Control Procurements is the process of managing procurement relationships, monitoring contract performance, and closing out contracts. BIdentify potential sellers Incorrect. The Plan Procurement Management process is where everything related to procurements is planned and developed. Make-or-buy decisions, procurement strategy, bid documents, the procurement statement of work and identifying potential sellers are all outputs of this process making this an incorrect answer. CReview sellers' responses Incorrect. Conduct Procurements is the process of obtaining seller responses, selecting sellers, and awarding contracts. Therefore, reviewing seller responses would be part of the Conduct Procurements process and not part of Control Procurements. DPerform source selection analysis Incorrect. Source selection analysis is a tool used during Plan Procurement Management to decide on an evaluation method that will be used for selecting sellers during the Conduct Procurements process. Since it is used in the selection of sellers and precedes any contract with sellers, it is not part of the Control Procurements process and an incorrect answer.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-24$proc29$;

-- Q25: PROC-25 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The scenario implies you are carrying out the Plan Procurement Management process. Research alone may not provide specific information to formulate a procurement strategy without additional information interchange meetings with potential bidders. By collaborating with potential bidders, the organization purchasing the material or services may benefit while the seller can influence a mutually beneficial approach or product. Meetings can be used to determine the strategy for managing and monitoring the procurement. Of the available answer choices, meetings would be the most helpful tool to gain input from potential sellers. Details for Each Option: ASource selection analysis Incorrect. Source selection analysis provides the evaluation methods to be used to select a vendor. Source selection criteria are communicated to the potential bidders as part of the bid documents. However, the question is asking about gaining input ‘from’ potential bidders rather than providing information ‘to’ them. BMeetings with prospective sellers Correct. Research alone may not provide specific information to formulate a procurement strategy without additional information interchange meetings with potential sellers. Meetings can be used to determine the strategy for managing and monitoring the procurement. CBidder conferences Incorrect. Bidder conferences are among the tools and techniques of the Conduct Procurements process, while the scenario implies you are carrying out the Plan Procurement Management process DMake-or-buy analysis Incorrect. Make-or-buy analysis is the process of gathering and organizing data about product requirements and analyzing them against available alternatives including the purchase or internal manufacture of the product. The question states that vendors will be needed to provide services for the project. Therefore, it is apparent that the make-or-buy decision has already been made.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-25$proc29$;

-- Q26: PROC-26 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$Larger projects may use an adaptive approach for some deliverables and a traditional project management approach for other parts. In these cases, a governing agreement such as a master services agreement (MSA) may be used for the overall engagement, with the adaptive work being placed in an appendix or supplement. This hybrid strategy allows changes to occur on the adaptive scope without impacting the overall contract. The incorrect answer choices represent actions that will not permit the selected vendor from using the best project management approach for each aspect of the project. Since both vendors are capable of delivering both the hardware and the software, and since there is not enough information in the question to determine which one of them is better at delivering projects using the waterfall or agile project management approach, among the available choices, utilizing a master services agreement (MSA) with either of the vendors provides the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: AContract with vendor X for hardware, with vendor Y for software and use the appropriate methodology for each Incorrect. This approach represents a course of action that resolves the issue of which project management methodology to use. However, since both vendors are equally capable of producing both the hardware and the software, it is better to make appropriate arrangements to allow one vendor to deliver both. It may save project resources in managing one vendor instead of two. BContract with vendor X for both hardware and software and require an agile framework for the entire project Incorrect. As stated by the scenario, it has been concluded that an agile framework is best for developing the software component of the project, but not the hardware. Limiting the vendor to use one approach over another would diminish the benefits of using the best project management approach for each aspect of the project. CContract with vendor Y for both hardware and software and require a waterfall approach for the entire project Incorrect. According to the question text, a traditional waterfall project management approach is best to develop the hardware for the project in this case, but not the software component. Limiting the vendor to use one approach over another would diminish the benefits of using the best project management approach for each aspect of the project. DContract with either vendor X or vendor Y for both hardware and software and utilize a master services agreement Correct. In this case, a governing agreement such as a master services agreement (MSA) may be used with any of the vendors described in the scenario for the overall engagement, with the adaptive work being placed in an appendix or supplement.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-26$proc29$;

-- Q27: PROC-27 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The project manager should ensure that vendors are selected objectively by establishing a fair and objective vendor proposal evaluation process. Although using the established source selection criteria will set objective selection standards, it may be unrealistic for the source selection criteria to cover every nuance of the submitted proposals. Thus, even with the use of the source selection criteria, the risk of personal biases influencing the vendor selection process may still exist. By removing any identifying information from the proposals and using the established source selection criteria, the project manager can ensure that each proposal is evaluated objectively and fairly. Therefore, of the available options, the best course of action for the project manager is to remove vendors' identifying information from their proposals and let the committee evaluate the proposals against the source selection criteria. Details for Each Option: ARemove vendors' identifying information from their proposals and let the committee evaluate the proposals against the source selection criteria Correct. The seller proposals should be evaluated objectively based on the merits of the proposals rather than personal biases. Removing any identifying information from the proposals would eliminate any influence from personal biases. BHold the meeting as scheduled and let the committee evaluate the vendors' proposals against the source selection criteria Incorrect. Holding the meeting as scheduled would ignore the fact that some of the committee members are biased. This situation may result in selecting the vendor that is not properly vetted against the source selection criteria and therefore is not the best for the project, thus reducing the chances of project success. CEscalate the issue to the project sponsor since preconceived opinions of the committee members are outside the control of the project manager Incorrect. While it is true that personal preferences of the committee members are likely outside the control of the project manager, there are proactive steps that can be done by the project manager before the issue should be escalated to the project sponsor. DCancel the meeting to avoid bias and select the vendor based on the merits of the proposals and the source selection criteria Incorrect. Although making the decision without the participation of the evaluation committee would address the issue of personal biases, bypassing the committee would violate the organizational policy that requires vendors to be selected by an evaluation committee.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-27$proc29$;

-- Q28: PROC-28 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The scenario described in the question implies the project team is performing the Conduct Procurements process. The source selection criteria is an output of the Plan Procurement Management process which then becomes an input to the Conduct Procurements process. The project manager should understand the established selection criteria before making a decision regarding a vendor. Details for Each Option: AReview the source selection criteria Correct. The source selection criteria should be used to rate or score the seller proposals. BSelect the lowest cost vendor Incorrect. The cost is just one factor that should be considered. CReview the requirements traceability matrix Incorrect. The requirements traceability matrix is a document that links product requirements from their origin to the deliverables that satisfy them. Unlike the requirements traceability matrix, the source selection criteria describe the criteria which will be used to evaluate bidder proposals. DSelect the vendor with the most expertise Incorrect. The expertise of the vendor is just one factor that should be considered.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-28$proc29$;

-- Q29: PROC-29 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$When being faced with procurement-related questions on the PMP exam, a prospective PMP aspirant has first to determine what side of the procurement the scenario is talking about. While the majority of procurement-related questions on the exam describe the situation from a buyer's point of view, some questions are worded from the seller's perspective. This question is an example of the latter: you are the seller (or a bidder, a vendor). Therefore, some of the answer choices can be eliminated as they refer to processes, inputs, outputs, tools or techniques that are buyer-related. As it pertains to the scenario described, it is a good practice for buyers to include their selected evaluation method in the procurement documents, so bidders know how they will be evaluated. Commonly used selection methods include least cost, qualifications only, quality-based/highest technical proposal score, quality and cost-based, sole source, or fixed budget. To position your company as the best fit for the project, it is essential for you to understand the requirements and selection criteria that you need to meet as a seller. Therefore, of the choices provided, looking for the selection method in the procurement documents of the utility company's bid package is the best answer to the question asked. Note, the complete source selection criteria is not always provided by the buyer to the seller. These criteria are often internal for the buying organization. However, some subset of the criteria can be exposed to the prospective sellers to provide them with a better understanding of what the buyer is looking for besides the technical details of the solution. Details for Each Option: AAttend the bid conference to learn all you need to know about the utility company's requirements. Incorrect. Without first thoroughly reading the bid package, you would not be prepared to ask useful questions or even assessing whether your company meets the minimum requirements. BRequest an independent cost estimate to include in your seller response. Incorrect. Independent cost estimates are typically developed by the buying organization, while in the scenario described you are the seller. CLook for the selection method in the procurement documents of the utility company's bid package. Correct. The bid package from the buyer should provide you as a seller all the requirements and selection criteria that you need to meet. DRe-use a proposal that won selection by a telecommunications company. Incorrect. If your project management office does not provide a bid or proposal template, an existing proposal may be used as a basis, but you must be careful not to break any non-disclosure or intellectual property agreements with the previous company. Then again, a proposal for a telecommunications project may not be relevant to the solar energy project.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-29$proc29$;

-- Q30: PROC-30 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$According to the scenario, the Control Procurements process has already been completed. Three of the four answer choices represent activities, which are typically performed during the Control Procurements process and, therefore, can be eliminated. Measuring stakeholder satisfaction is part of the Close Project or Phase process. While the scenario does not explicitly mention or implicitly suggests that the project/phase is in closure, of the choices provided, after completing the activities associated with the Control Procurements process, measuring stakeholder satisfaction is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: APay the vendor's invoice Incorrect. Paying vendor invoices is part of the Control Procurements process which is, according to the scenario described, has already been completed. BApprove the deliverable received from the vendor Incorrect. According to the scenario described, the project manager has completed the Control Procurements process. Therefore, deliverables received from the vendor should have already been approved. CConduct an audit Incorrect. A procurement audit is a structured review of the procurement process. An audit is one of the tools and techniques used in the Control Procurement process which is, according to the scenario described, has already been completed. DMeasure stakeholder satisfaction Correct. One of the activities necessary for the administrative closure of the project or phase includes measuring stakeholder satisfaction. Stakeholders' feedback can lead to improved processes and procedures for future projects. Of the available choices, only measuring stakeholder satisfaction is the next logical step in this situation.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-30$proc29$;

-- Q31: PROC-31 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$Disputes and miscommunications between buyers and sellers can happen. Some unintentional or by mistake. The additional bills from the service provider to the project may be planned or in error. Until the project manager confirms the situation and that no further billing to the project should occur, the appropriate actions are still not fully known. In this scenario, it could be part of the procurement agreement that the bills are spread out over the course of the project and not only at the point of delivery. Or it could be that the automatic billing activity was to stop once the final services were delivered. Gathering information and understanding the root of the problem is the best option for the project manager to take in this situation. Details for Each Option: AConfirm that no more services are needed and the procurement agreements are closed Correct. Before taking action on questionable procurement documents, the project manager ensures that no services are required or being requested by the project. Confirming the state of the procurement agreements enables the project manager to make the appropriate response. BClose all agreements with all service providers engaged with the project Incorrect. There may be multiple open agreements with several service providers. Closing all the agreements may close procurement arrangements that are still active and required by the project to meet its objectives. CForward the service bills to the project management office for further review and approval Incorrect. The purpose of the project management office (PMO) is not to handle payments and contract disputes with suppliers. The PMO can provide direction and resources, but handing off the responsibility to the PMO is not the best option in most organizations. DContinue paying the bills until the project is formally closed to avoid claims Incorrect. Until the project manager confirms that the payments are to continue, the payments should not be paid blindly until the project is closed. Procurements can be closed while the project still goes on. If agreements between the parties specify so, then the payment should continue after confirmation of those terms. Otherwise, the payments should stop provided the project manager confirmed that no more services are needed, and the procurement agreements are closed.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-31$proc29$;

-- Q32: PROC-32 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The scenario mixes up two processes: the Close Project or Phase process and the Control Procurements process. As part of the Close Project or Phase process, you need to finalize all open claims, while an output from the Control Procurements process is closed procurements. However, procurements cannot be closed until all open claims are finalized which is an activity of the Close Project or Phase process. In this scenario, there is a dispute in the claim, and direct negotiations between your company and the vendor have been unsuccessful. Whenever a settlement cannot be achieved through negotiation, some form of alternative dispute resolution (ADR) including mediation or arbitration should be explored. Claims administration, such as ADR, can be used as a tool or technique during the Control Procurements process. Of the choices provided, seeking alternative dispute resolution is the best thing for you to do in this situation and is, therefore, the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: APay the vendor to avoid a conflict Incorrect. Before the project can be closed, you need to ensure all vendors are paid. However, you only pay vendors if the work meets formal acceptance criteria. In this scenario, the final deliverable does not meet the acceptance criteria. Therefore, paying the vendor to avoid the conflict would not resolve the issue with the deliverable, Worse, it will show the vendor you have accepted their work, and the objectives of your project will not be achieved. BAppoint a lawyer and go to court Incorrect. Whenever a settlement can't be achieved through negotiation, some form of alternative dispute resolution (ADR) including mediation or arbitration should be explored. Appointing a lawyer and going to court would be your last resort. CIgnore the vendor until they agree to your demands Incorrect. As part of the Close Project or Phase process, you need to finalize all open claims. Ignoring the vendor may or may not result in the vendor agreeing to your demands. However, it is likely that ignoring the vendor will only prolong the problem. You should try to resolve the dispute proactively instead of waiting for the desired outcome that may never occur. DSeek alternative dispute resolution Correct. To close the project or phase properly, you need to finalize all open claims. In this scenario, there is a dispute in the claim, and negotiations have been unsuccessful. Whenever a settlement can't be achieved through negotiation, some form of alternative dispute resolution (ADR) including mediation or arbitration should be explored.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-32$proc29$;

-- Q33: PROC-33 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$When planning the procurement needs of the project and how to manage those needs, understanding the organization’s policies and procedures frames what one can and cannot do or what one is encouraged to follow. In this circumstance, the organization has placed some requirements on the commodities (which are common, standard items) purchased by projects. Consequently, when planning the management of the project procurement, this information shapes the source selection options and its analysis because certain items can only be sourced from certain suppliers (sellers). Note, the full name of the process is Plan Procurement Management. While the correct answer provides only a partial name of the process, 'Plan Procurement', of the choices provided, 'Source selection in Plan Procurement' is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: ASource selection in Plan Procurement Correct. When the project manager and team – say the procurement manager – are analyzing the procurement needs of the project and determining source selection, the inclusion of such factors as least cost and sole source are extremely important in preparing the procurement plans. BData representation in Identify Stakeholders Incorrect. Stakeholders are not commodities. Those who supply commodities to the project may be considered stakeholders to the project, but the criteria listed is focusing more on the selection of those procurement needs rather than the analyzing of the stakeholders. CPre-assignment in Acquire Resources Incorrect. Resources may be acquired for the project from outside sellers, but pre-assignment is not about those resources procured by the project. Pre-assignment is where resources provided to the project with little or no influence from the project team. DAccepted deliverables in Validate Scope Incorrect. In the process of Validate Scope, the stakeholders are verifying and accepting the deliverables, not analyzing the sources of the materials and whether they should or should not be purchased from various sources like the least cost supplier or the sole source of the material.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-33$proc29$;

-- Q34: PROC-34 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The question suggests that you, as the project manager, are conducting the Control Procurements process, and you are seeking information about a vendor's performance from past projects. Even though you do not have any history with this vendor, the fact that the PMO has listed this vendor as a preapproved seller suggests that the vendor may have been used on past projects. It is possible that this vendor is known for starting slow and consistently finishing ahead of schedule. This information might give you more confidence in the vendor's ability to deliver as contracted. Ideally, you would review the lessons learned repository which is an organizational process asset that contains knowledge gained from past projects. However, reviewing the lesson's learned repository is not listed as an answer choice. The PMO should be able to provide best practices, access to information, and lessons learned from past projects. Of the available options, the best source of information regarding the vendor's performance on past projects is the PMO particularly since the vendor is a preapproved seller. Note that neither the lessons learned repository nor the PMO is listed as an input to the Control Procurements process, but the wording of the PMBOK® Guide always states "including but not limited to" when referencing inputs, outputs, or tools and techniques. Therefore, sometimes the PMP aspirant must look beyond what is specifically listed in the PMBOK® Guide and select the option that best answers the question asked. Details for Each Option: ALook in the lessons learned register Incorrect. The lessons learned register is a project document used to record knowledge gained during the current project so that it can be used in the project. The question indicates that you, as the project manager, do not have any prior experience with the vendor on the current project. Therefore, it is not likely that any knowledge has been gained regarding this vendor in the project. BReview the risk response plan in the risk register Incorrect. The risk that the vendor may not finish on time should be captured in the risk register along with a planned risk response. However, the question is asked where you might find more information regarding the performance of this vendor, and the risk register is not likely to have this information. CCheck with the PMO and see what information they have on the vendor Correct. A PMO may be able to provide best practices, access to information, and lessons learned from past projects. Even though you, as the project manager, do not have any prior experience with this vendor, the fact that the PMO had them listed as a preapproved seller suggests that the vendor may have been used on past projects. DDiscuss your concerns with the project sponsor Incorrect. While there is nothing wrong in discussing your concerns with the project sponsor, the project sponsor may or may not have the information about the past performance of the vendor, making this choice, of those provided, not the best answer to the question asked.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-34$proc29$;

-- Q35: PROC-35 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The question implies that the Conduct Procurements was being performed when it came to light that there were no available vendors capable of performing the required work. Even though the source selection criteria are not determined until project planning is performed, the basic requirements of the project and timeline should be understood during project initiation. Because each project is unique, the project manager should tailor the way that Project Procurement Management processes are applied. Tailoring considerations include, among others, the availability of contractors. Had the project manager considered the availability of contractors before completing the procurement management plan, time and effort could have been directed towards planning to perform the required work internally, which even if it costs more than outsourcing, in the absence of available vendors, is the only viable option to completing the project on time. Details for Each Option: AIncluded the evaluation method in the procurement documents Incorrect. It is a project management best practice to include the evaluation method in the procurement documents, so the prospective bidders know how they will be evaluated. However, including the evaluation method in the procurement documents would not have changed the fact that there are no vendors available who are available to perform the required work. BObtained an independent cost estimate Incorrect. The procuring organization may elect to prepare an independent estimate to serve as a benchmark for proposed responses. However, in this scenario, there are no available vendors capable of performing the required work. Therefore, there would not be any proposals to evaluate. CTailored the source selection criteria to include vendors' availability Correct. Tailoring considerations include, among others, the availability of contractors. Had the project manager considered the availability of contractors before completing the procurement management plan, time and effort could have been directed towards planning to perform the required work internally. DConducted a make-or-buy analysis Incorrect. The question suggests that a make-or-buy analysis was performed and it would be more cost-effective to contract with a vendor than to perform the work with internal resources. The availability of vendors is not typically addressed as part of the make-or-buy analysis.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-35$proc29$;

-- Q36: PROC-36 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The question implies that the project manager is performing the Conduct Procurements process. One of the trends and emerging practices associated with Project Procurement Management is represented by changing contracting processes. International contracts with multiple contractors from many countries are inherently riskier than projects using only local contractors. For these projects, the use of internationally recognized standard contract forms is increasing to reduce problems and claims during administration. Therefore, of the available answer choices, the project manager should consider using internationally recognized standard contract forms in this scenario. Details for Each Option: AAvoid using bidder conferences due to international travel expenses Incorrect. Bidder conferences are meetings with prospective sellers prior to the preparation of a bid or proposal to ensure all prospective vendors have a clear and common understanding of the procurement. Contractors may or may not be willing to pay for international travel to attend a bidder conference. The project manager may consider virtual options for bidder conferences to avoid international travel but avoiding bidder conferences would not reduce claims during execution. BUse internationally recognized standard contract forms Correct. The use of internationally recognized standard contract forms should be considered to reduce problems and claims during the administration of a project involving multiple contractors from various countries. CUtilize a preapproved seller list Incorrect. A preapproved seller list is a list of sellers that have been properly vetted, which can streamline the steps needed to advertise the opportunity and shorten the timeline for the seller selection process. A preapproved seller list may or may not be used when dealing with international contractors. However, utilizing a preapproved seller list is not likely to reduce claims during execution. DAuthorize the CCB to handle claims administration Incorrect. A change control board (CCB) is a formally chartered group responsible for reviewing, evaluating, approving, delaying, or rejecting changes to the project. A CCB would not be involved in claims administration.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-36$proc29$;

-- Q37: PROC-37 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The Control Procurements process has a financial management component that involves monitoring payments to the seller. One of the chief concerns is making sure the charges from the seller represent the work that has been completed. By auditing the seller invoices, you can ensure the seller is charging you correctly for the work that has been completed. In this scenario, the audit should help ease the sponsor's concerns of being overcharged by the seller. Details for Each Option: AYou audit every invoice to ensure the costs are applicable and chargeable to your project. Correct. A cost-reimbursable contract involves payments (cost reimbursements) to the seller for all costs incurred for completed work. By auditing every invoice, you can ensure that the costs that have been billed are aligned with the work that has been completed and any extra charges will be investigated. This proactive review should put the sponsor at ease regarding the cost-reimbursable contract. BYou do not expect any changes to the scope. Incorrect. A cost-reimbursable contract involves payments (cost reimbursements) to the seller for all costs incurred for completed work. This type of agreement is used when the scope of the work is expected to change, and costs cannot be estimated accurately enough to use a fixed-price contract, which has less buyer cost risk. If you did not expect changes to the scope, you would likely have negotiated a fixed-price contract. Furthermore, saying that you do not expect 'any' changes to the scope is likely to be unrealistic even with fixed-price contracts. CThe seller has the risk with this type of contract. Incorrect. A cost-reimbursable contract involves payments (cost reimbursements) to the seller for all costs incurred for completed work. In this type of agreement, you, as the buyer, have the most cost risk because the total costs are unknown. DWith this type of contract, the seller agrees to a set reimbursement amount, so the final costs to your project are pre-determined. Incorrect. This answer is describing a fixed-price contract where a fixed total price is set for the work while in the scenario described you have a cost-reimbursable contract which involves payments (cost reimbursements) to the seller for all costs incurred for completed work.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-37$proc29$;

-- Q38: PROC-38 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The question implies that the Conduct Procurements process is being performed. To evaluate the proposals of the prospective vendors during that process, a data analysis technique can be used. Proposals are evaluated to ensure they are complete and respond in full to the bid documents, procurement statement of work, source selection criteria, and any other documents that went out in the bid package. Additionally, of the available choices, only data analysis is a tool or technique used in the Conduct Procurements process. Details for Each Option: AData analysis Correct. A data analysis technique that can be used during the Conduct Procurements process includes proposal evaluation. Proposals are evaluated to ensure they are complete and respond in full to the bid documents, procurement statement of work, source selection criteria, and any other documents that went out in the bid package. BPerformance reviews Incorrect. Performance reviews analyze the performance of a vendor against the contract, as opposed to analyzing the proposal submitted by the vendor. CAudits Incorrect. Audits are a structured review of the procurement process. Since audits are used to evaluate the procurement process rather than vendors’ proposals, this choice can be eliminated as a correct answer. DInspection Incorrect. Inspection is a structured review of the work being performed by the vendor, rather than an evaluation of the proposal submitted by the vendor.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-38$proc29$;

-- Q39: PROC-39 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$Of the available options, only the procurement file and the seller performance evaluation documentation are organizational process assets. Seller performance evaluation documentation is prepared by the buyer and documents the seller's ability to continue to perform work on the current contract, indicates whether the seller can be allowed to perform work on future projects, or rates how well the seller is performing the project work or has performed in the past. Additionally, if the vendor was listed as a preapproved seller, then the prequalified seller list should be updated to remove the vendor from being preapproved for future work, but the prequalified seller list was not listed as an answer choice. Therefore, of the choices provided, seller performance evaluation documentation is the best answer to the question asked. Details for Each Option: AProcurement management plan Incorrect. The procurement management plan is not updated with performance issues of vendors. Additionally, the procurement management plan is a component of the project management plan and not an organizational process asset. BProcurement file Incorrect. The procurement file contains a complete set of indexed contract documentation. While the procurement file is an organizational process asset, it does not include information on seller performance, and, therefore, is not the best answer to the question asked. CLessons learned register Incorrect. The lessons learned register might be updated with details on issues encountered during a procurement audit. However, the lessons learned register is a project document and not an organizational process asset. DSeller performance evaluation documentation Correct. Seller performance evaluation documentation is prepared by the buyer and documents the seller's ability to continue to perform work on the current contract, indicates whether the seller can be allowed to perform work on future projects, or rates how well the seller is performing the project work or has performed in the past. Also, the question implies that the Control Procurements process is carried out in the scenario, and seller performance evaluation documentation is one of the organizational process assets that may be updated during that process.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-39$proc29$;

-- Q40: PROC-40 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The scenario implies the project is in the Plan Procurement Management process. When some aspects of the procurement are well-defined up-front but others are unknown, the parties to the contract may use a multi-tiered contract structure. This contracting technique allows for capturing various aspects of the procurement in different documents. For example, mostly fixed items, such as warranties and arbitration, can be locked in a master services agreement (MSA), while more dynamic items, such as scope, schedule, and budget, can be formalized in a lightweight statement of work. While the scenario does not provide specific details as to what items are fixed and what are variable, of the choices provided, the multi-tiered structure best addresses the question asked. This structure reflects a hybrid project management approach in which part of the procurement is managed using predictive methods, while the other part is addressed with agile contracting techniques. Details for Each Option: AMulti-tiered structure Correct. A multi-tiered structure is an agile or hybrid contracting technique that best works for procurements in which some aspects are well-defined up-front while others are unknown. This technique uses a master services agreement (MSA) to document fixed items and a lightweight statement of work to capture those that are yet to be discovered. BCost plus award fee Incorrect. Cost plus award fee is an example of a cost-reimbursable contract in which the seller is reimbursed based on specific criteria. This choice is an incorrect answer for two reasons: first, cost plus award fee is a contract, not a contracting technique, as asked by the question. Second, it does not address the essence of the question, which focuses on how to contractually handle a procurement that has requirements of a various degree of clarity. CProcurement audit Incorrect. A procurement audit is one of the tools and techniques of the Control Procurements process. The audit is a structured review of the procurement process rather than a contracting technique used to address the uncertainty of requirements during the Plan Procurement Management process, as described by the scenario. DRequest for quotation Incorrect. Request for quotation (RFQ) is an example of a procurement document that is used as part of the Plan Procurement Management process to request price quotations from prospective sellers of common or standard goods or services. In the scenario, some of the goods/services are unknown and, hence, are not considered common or standard.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-40$proc29$;

-- Q41: PROC-41 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The scenario describes a project in the closing stage. Procurements should be closed, and all claims settled before the project can be closed. Procurements are closed as part of the Control Procurements process. Claims administration is one of the tools and techniques of this process. In all procurement relationships, the ideal method of finalizing all outstanding issues, claims, and disputes is through negotiation. In the scenario described, the project manager does not know if the $30,000 difference between the invoice and the project manager's calculation is a mistake or an issue. Therefore, talking directly to the contractor is the easiest and fastest way to determine the root cause of the discrepancy. Whenever a settlement cannot be achieved through direct negotiation between a buyer and a seller, some form of alternative dispute resolution (ADR), such as mediation or arbitration may be explored. When all else fails, litigation in the courts is possible but is considered the least desirable option. Details for Each Option: APay the contractor $70,000 Incorrect. Based on the scenario, the project manager does not agree with the contractor's invoiced amount. Short paying the contractor is not going to resolve it. You should talk to the contractor to understand the reason for the discrepancy and come up with a mutual agreement. BTalk to the contractor Correct. The scenario suggests there is a procurement dispute between the project manager and the contractor. The $30,000 difference between the invoice and the project manager's calculation could be a mistake. Therefore, the easiest and fastest way to begin resolution is by talking to the contractor directly. In other words, in all procurement relationships the ideal method of finalizing outstanding issues, claims, and disputes is through direct negotiation between the involved parties. Even if the discrepancy turns out to be real, direct negotiations are still the preferred method to settle the dispute. CUse alternative dispute resolution (ADR) Incorrect. The project manager should first try to resolve the dispute via negotiation. If the settlement cannot be achieved through direct negotiation between a buyer and a seller, some form of alternative dispute resolution (ADR), such as mediation or arbitration may be explored. DTake the contractor to court Incorrect. Taking the contractor to court is the last resort and the least preferred method to resolve procurement disputes. However, if negotiations and alternative dispute resolution fail, this choice represents an action that the project manager will have to take eventually.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-41$proc29$;

-- Q42: PROC-42 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$In this scenario, you are a project manager at the company executing the project. Your contract with the customer includes specific conditions to ensure that the IP for the design is protected. You have outsourced the design work to a vendor. Unfortunately, your contract with the vendor does not specify the conditions that establish and protect the customer's IP. The vendor has done nothing wrong by submitting the previous design to your company, and nothing in the question indicates that you have either discussed or by contract bound the vendor to protect the client's IP. Therefore, it was a lapse on the part of your company not to inform or contractually bind the vendor to the same IP restrictions that your company agreed to protect. You need to use your company's contract change control system to modify the contract to include the IP considerations of your customer and present the amendment to your vendor for approval. You also have to request the vendor to redesign the component since its already out in the market and used by the other client. Details for Each Option: AInclude the vendor in client meetings and make them realize the importance of protecting intellectual property Incorrect. The vendor likely understands the importance of protecting IP in general. However, the contract with the vendor does not include an IP clause. Hence the vendor is not obligated to protect your customer's IP. Therefore, including the vendor in client meetings without adding the IP clause into the contract is unlikely to resolve the issue at hand. BCancel the contract with the vendor and request monetary compensation for the damage Incorrect. Nothing in the scenario indicates that the vendor is bound by an IP clause or is sharing a design that is protected by the intellectual property of another client. Canceling the contract with the vendor is unsubstantiated, let alone requesting monetary compensation for something that is not the vendor's responsibility. CShow the designs to the customer to get approval before beginning the manufacturing of the components Incorrect. Showing the designs to the customer to get approval before beginning the manufacturing of the components is unethical; you know the designs are not unique and violate the contract with the customer. DUse the contract change control system and request the vendor to redesign the component Correct. The contract change control system is used to collect, track, arbitrate, and communicate changes to a contract. Since the design submitted by the vendor violates one of the IP restrictions in the contract with the customer, informing the vendor about the IP protection clause with the customer, updating the existing contract, and requesting the vendor to redesign the component is the best course of action for the project manager.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-42$proc29$;

-- Q43: PROC-43 explanation
UPDATE questions
SET explanation = $proc29$The third value of the Agile Manifesto is "Customer collaboration over contract negotiation." Customer collaboration forms the basis for the joint decision-making process. It establishes a bond between the customer and the development team, helping ensure the customer receives the intended benefits of the project. The question describes a situation where the vendor insists that product specifications are thoroughly documented in the contract even though the buyer (customer) does not have a clear picture of the requirements upfront. The vendor produces the deliverables according to the contract negotiated at the beginning of the project, but the product does not deliver the intended value/result. In some cases, a buyer is not a product expert and relies on the vendor to guide the buyer in the product design and development. This approach is an example of collaborating with the customer over rigidly adhering to specifications outlined in a contract. Details for Each Option: ABusiness people and developers must work together Incorrect. This principle may have prevented the problem outlined in the scenario from occurring. However, this answer choice represents a principle of the Agile Manifesto rather than one of its values. BIndividuals and interactions over processes and tools Incorrect. There is not enough information in the question to determine if processes and tools were valued over individuals and interactions, resulting in the buyer's dissatisfaction with the deliverables. Better collaboration between the parties would have had a more positive effect on the outcome of the project. CPair programming over continuous integration Incorrect. Pair programming is a technique in which two developers work side-by-side developing software code. Continuous integration is a practice in which each team member's work products are frequently integrated and validated with one another. Both are valuable agile techniques; however, it is unclear if applying/valuing one of them over another would have prevented the problem described in the scenario from occurring in the first place. DCustomer collaboration over contract negotiations Correct. Thoroughly negotiating contracts is typical for projects that are lead using the traditional approach. In the scenario, however, the customer does not have detailed requirements upfront and requests that the vendor develops the device using agile methods. Collaboration over negotiations would be more beneficial for both parties.$proc29$,
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE question_id = $proc29$PROC-43$proc29$;

